╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Broken Function Level Authorization via Client-Supplied Header on    │
│  /admin                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 5.3                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-node                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /admin                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `/admin` endpoint relies solely on a client-supplied HTTP header        │
│  (`x-admin`) to authorize administrative access. An unauthenticated          │
│  attacker can supply `x-admin: 1` to bypass authorization checks and access  │
│  the restricted admin panel.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Allows unauthorized users to access administrative endpoints and sensitive  │
│  application panels by forging HTTP headers.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The FastAPI application defines the `/admin` endpoint accepting an          │
│  `x-admin` header via `Header(default="")`. It performs a direct string      │
│  comparison `if x_admin == "1":` to grant access. Because HTTP headers can   │
│  be arbitrarily manipulated by clients, this represents a broken function    │
│  level authorization vulnerability.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a GET request to `/admin` without headers and observe a 403         │
│  Forbidden response.                                                         │
│  2. Send a GET request to `/admin` with the header `x-admin: 1` and observe  │
│  a 200 OK response with administrative panel content.                        │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  url = "http://127.0.0.1:8002/admin"                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  # Without header                                                            │
│  res = requests.get(url)                                                     │
│  print("Without header:", res.status_code, res.text)                         │
│                                                                              │
│  # With header                                                               │
│  res = requests.get(url, headers={"x-admin": "1"})                           │
│  print("With header:", res.status_code, res.text)                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:49-54                                                  │
│    Remove client-supplied header authorization bypass                        │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - @app.get("/admin")                                                      │
│  def admin(x_admin: str = Header(default="")):                               │
│      # VULN 4: header-trust authorization                                    │
│      if x_admin == "1":                                                      │
│          return {"panel": "ok", "note": "admin gated by a client-supplied    │
│  header"}                                                                    │
│      raise HTTPException(403, "forbidden")                                   │
│    + @app.get("/admin")                                                      │
│  def admin():                                                                │
│      raise HTTPException(403, "forbidden")                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Enforce authorization on the server side using robust session management,   │
│  tokens (such as JWTs), or secure role-based access control rather than      │
│  relying on client-supplied headers.                                         │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in /item endpoint                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 9.1                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-node                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /item                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `/item` endpoint uses f-string interpolation to construct SQL queries,  │
│  allowing attackers to inject arbitrary SQL via the `id` parameter.          │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An attacker can read, modify, or delete database records, potentially       │
│  accessing sensitive data like the secret flag.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The application uses a FastAPI endpoint `/item` that accepts an `id`        │
│  parameter. This parameter is directly interpolated into a SQL query         │
│  string: `f"SELECT id, name, secret FROM items WHERE id = {id}"`. Since      │
│  there is no validation or escaping, an attacker can break out of the        │
│  intended query and execute arbitrary SQL commands.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a request to `/item` with a valid ID to establish a baseline.       │
│  2. Send a request with `id=1%20AND%201=1` to confirm boolean-based          │
│  injection.                                                                  │
│  3. Send a request with `id=1%20AND%201=2` to confirm the negative case.     │
│  4. Use a `UNION SELECT` payload to extract arbitrary data.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│  url = "http://127.0.0.1:8001/item"                                          │
│  payload = "-1 UNION SELECT 1,2,3"                                           │
│  r = requests.get(url, params={"id": payload})                               │
│  print(r.json())                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:26-28                                                  │
│    SQL query construction                                                    │
│        c = db()                                                              │
│      rows = c.execute(f"SELECT id, name, secret FROM items WHERE id =        │
│  {id}").fetchall()                                                           │
│      return {"rows": rows}                                                   │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     c = db()                                                            │
│      rows = c.execute(f"SELECT id, name, secret FROM items WHERE id =        │
│  {id}").fetchall()                                                           │
│      return {"rows": rows}                                                   │
│    +     c = db()                                                            │
│      rows = c.execute("SELECT id, name, secret FROM items WHERE id = ?",     │
│  (id,)).fetchall()                                                           │
│      return {"rows": rows}                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) instead of string           │
│  concatenation or interpolation when building SQL queries.                   │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Path Traversal in /file endpoint via name parameter                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-node                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /file                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The /file endpoint in the FastAPI application is vulnerable to path         │
│  traversal due to improper validation of user-supplied input in the 'name'   │
│  parameter. An attacker can use '../' sequences to read arbitrary files on   │
│  the server.                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Arbitrary file read on the server, potentially leading to disclosure of     │
│  sensitive system files (e.g., /etc/passwd), application source code,        │
│  configuration files, or secrets.                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The endpoint constructs a file path by directly joining user-supplied       │
│  'name' parameter with a base directory using os.path.join() without         │
│  validating that the resulting path remains within the intended directory.   │
│  This allows directory traversal attacks.                                    │
│  Specifically, line 42: `path = os.path.join("/tmp/test-vuln-node/data",     │
│  name)` followed by opening and reading the file without path validation.    │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send a GET request to /file?name=../../../../etc/passwd                  │
│  2. Observe that the server returns the contents of /etc/passwd              │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│  response =                                                                  │
│  requests.get('http://127.0.0.1:8001/file?name=../../../../etc/passwd')      │
│  print(response.text)                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:42-44                                                  │
│    Vulnerable path construction and file read                                │
│        path = os.path.join("/tmp/test-vuln-node/data", name)                 │
│      try:                                                                    │
│          return PlainTextResponse(open(path).read())                         │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     path = os.path.join("/tmp/test-vuln-node/data", name)               │
│      try:                                                                    │
│          return PlainTextResponse(open(path).read())                         │
│    +     base_dir = "/tmp/test-vuln-node/data"                               │
│      path = os.path.abspath(os.path.join(base_dir, name))                    │
│      if not path.startswith(os.path.abspath(base_dir)):                      │
│          raise HTTPException(403, "Forbidden")                               │
│      try:                                                                    │
│          return PlainTextResponse(open(path).read())                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Validate user input to ensure the constructed path stays within the         │
│  intended directory. Use os.path.abspath() to resolve the absolute path and  │
│  check that it starts with the base directory before opening the file.       │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

